{"name":"UFO Defense Green","hex":"#88aa11","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ufo","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A UFO."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ufo"},{"term":"defense","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The action of defending or protecting from attack, danger, or injury.","Anything employed to oppose attack(s).\n A strategy and tactics employed to prevent the other team from scoring; contrasted with offense.\n The portion of a team dedicated to preventing the other team from scoring; contrasted with offense."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To employ defensive tactics (so as to block). .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/defense"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"UFO: Enemy Unknown","blocks":[{"text":"UFO: Enemy Unknown, also known as X-COM: UFO Defense in North America, is a 1994 science fiction strategy video game developed by Mythos Games and MicroProse. It was published by MicroProse for DOS and Amiga computers, the Amiga CD32 console, and the PlayStation. Originally planned by Julian Gollop as a sequel to Mythos Games' 1988 Laser Squad, the game mixes real-time management simulation with turn-based tactics. The player takes the role of commander of X-COM – an international paramilitary and scientific organisation secretly defending Earth from an alien invasion. Through the game, the player is tasked with issuing orders to individual X-COM troops in a series of turn-based tactical missions. At strategic scale, the player directs the research and development of new technologies, builds and expands X-COM's bases, manages the organisation's finances and personnel, and monitors and responds to UFO activity."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/66/UFO_Enemy_Unknown_cover_art.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UFO%3A_Enemy_Unknown","wikidata":"Q1261715"},{"title":"Unidentified flying object","blocks":[{"text":"An unidentified flying object (UFO) is an object or phenomenon seen in the sky but not yet identified or explained. The term was coined when United States Air Force (USAF) investigations into flying saucers found too broad a range of shapes reported to consider them all saucers or discs. UFOs are also known as unidentified aerial phenomena or unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAPs). Upon investigation, most UFOs are identified as known objects, natural phenomena, hoaxes, or mirages, while a small number remain unexplained."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Gimbal_The_First_Official_UAP_Footage_from_the_USG_for_Public_Release.webm/330px--Gimbal_The_First_Official_UAP_Footage_from_the_USG_for_Public_Release.webm.jpg","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unidentified_flying_object","wikidata":"Q421"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/4/4c/UFO_Geoscape.jpg/250px-UFO_Geoscape.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/4c/UFO_Geoscape.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/4/4c/UFO_Geoscape.jpg/250px-UFO_Geoscape.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A gameplay screenshot of the continuous-time strategic scale Geoscape mode, with the game's main menu to the right","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUFO_Geoscape.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/71/Xcom2.png/330px-Xcom2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/71/Xcom2.png/330px-Xcom2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A gameplay screenshot of the turn-based Battlescape tactical combat mode, showing an Alien Terror-type mission in urban environment (Click on the image for a more detailed description.)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AXcom2.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Flying_Saucer_-_NARA_NAID_303938034_cropped.png/500px-Flying_Saucer_-_NARA_NAID_303938034_cropped.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Flying_Saucer_-_NARA_NAID_303938034_cropped.png/500px-Flying_Saucer_-_NARA_NAID_303938034_cropped.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlying_Saucer_-_NARA_NAID_303938034_cropped.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Fata_Morgana_Example.jpg/500px-Fata_Morgana_Example.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Fata_Morgana_Example.jpg/1280px-Fata_Morgana_Example.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Fata_Morgana_Example.jpg/500px-Fata_Morgana_Example.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Fata Morgana, a type of mirage in which objects located below the astronomical horizon appear to be hovering in the sky just above the horizon, may be responsible for some UFO sightings.[21]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFata_Morgana_Example.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/SpaceX_Falcon_9_Launch_from_Vandenberg%2C_CA_-_October_7_2018_-_45131925812.jpg/500px-SpaceX_Falcon_9_Launch_from_Vandenberg%2C_CA_-_October_7_2018_-_45131925812.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/SpaceX_Falcon_9_Launch_from_Vandenberg%2C_CA_-_October_7_2018_-_45131925812.jpg/1280px-SpaceX_Falcon_9_Launch_from_Vandenberg%2C_CA_-_October_7_2018_-_45131925812.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/SpaceX_Falcon_9_Launch_from_Vandenberg%2C_CA_-_October_7_2018_-_45131925812.jpg/500px-SpaceX_Falcon_9